<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
        DELIBERATELY EMPTY OF THEME DEFINITIONS.

        This file previously redefined Maui.MainTheme and Maui.MainTheme.NoActionBar
        so the Android system chrome could carry the app's gold/ink brand colours.
        That override forced the parent to Theme.MaterialComponents.DayNight.NoActionBar
        (Material 2).

        .NET 9/10 MAUI inflates Material 3 layouts internally. Running those
        layouts under a Material 2 theme makes view IDs and attributes resolve
        against the wrong library, which surfaces as inflation failures deep
        inside MAUI's own fragments — for example
        NavigationRootManager_ElementBasedFragment failing to find @id/jumpToStart,
        an ID that belongs to the Material library, not to this app.

        The app declares no layouts and no fragments of its own, so there is
        nothing here to "fix" in user code: the correct action is to stop
        overriding the theme and let the MAUI SDK supply Maui.MainTheme,
        Maui.MainTheme.NoActionBar and Maui.SplashTheme exactly as its own
        layouts expect.

        If the Android chrome colours are wanted back later, override the
        colour ATTRIBUTES only, inheriting from MAUI's theme rather than
        replacing its parent:

            <style name="Maui.MainTheme.NoActionBar" parent="Theme.Material3.DayNight.NoActionBar">

        …and verify on a device first. The in-app UI is fully custom-drawn, so
        the visible cost of not theming the system chrome is close to zero.
    -->
</resources>
